\f1\fs22 Chilo found Johnny sulking over a gold-leaf cappuccino that cost more than a waiter\'97usually an emaciated wraith hoping to break into modelling\'97made in the caf\'e9 in a month. The price of the coffee made a certain amount of economic sense, assuming one valued cruelty-free, organic, locally-sourced human excreta. It was brewed from beans shat from the arses of fashion models trying to make a little extra. It was easy money; the beans filled their bellies, and yielded almost no calories as long as they passed through fast enough. Johnny hadn\'92t touched the coffee in over fifteen minutes; it had turned tepid and looked like silty water in the birdbath of a cup. He was in a state of torpor, the kind born of depression and the fear of any movement making matters worse.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>None of these facts about Johnny\'92s coffee were immediately available to Chilo, who was standing outside and rapping on the window, trying to get Johnny\'92s attention. While Chilo was tolerated on this promenade so high above the Layer, the caf\'e9 admitted only Airborne clientele. To Chilo, Johnny appeared catatonic, so he rapped harder on the window which, of course, unsettled the clientele.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Excuse me,\'94 the waiter said to Johnny. \'93There appears to be a deranged old man outside, trying to get your attention.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What?\'94 Johnny asked, then looked at the waiter. Johnny took a moment processing what the waiter had said. He blinked, processed a little more, and summoned enough energy to turn his head towards the window. \'93Send him over,\'94 he said flatly. He didn\'92t care who he associated with any more, nor who saw who he was associating with.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sir?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You heard me,\'94 Johnny said without raising his voice, so perhaps the waiter hadn\'92t heard him. Perhaps they\'92d divined what Johnny wanted, because their shoulders slumped and they gave a queasy look as if Johnny had ordered them to pat a rat. Resigned to acquiescing to Johnny\'92s station in life, the waiter made busy to usher Chilo to the table, so they could be done with such unpleasantness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93May I sit?\'94 Chilo asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Who are you?\'94 Johnny asked, without a trace of verve.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo frowned, then with the showmanship of a magician, extracted a card from a pocket, and presented it to Johnny. Johnny failed to take it, so Chilo placed it on the table for Johnny to read.\
