<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well Mr\'85\'94 Johnny squinted at the card. \'93Damage eat us?\'94 He shifted his squint to Chilo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Close enough.\'94 Chilo shrugged. \'93My father\'92s name. It\'92s Greek.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, of course it is,\'94 Johnny said, recovering some of his know-it-all demeanour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo, assessing the greetings over, presumed to take the seat opposite Johnny. \'93Excuse me, waiter. Coffee please.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Without the gold leaf,\'94 Johnny added, believing this vagabond would stiff him for paying the bill. A little more alert, he scrutinised the old man: long beard, crazed skin, deep eyes, a cat\'92s grin that dripped of fang and mischief. \'93Wait\'85\'94 Johnny assembled some mental pieces. \'93You\'92re one of those lunatics who accosted my girlfriend\'85 at the Memphis Bar, such a disreputable place\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Memphis Mafia Bar,\'94 Chilo corrected. \'93And Sarah, isn\'92t your girlfriend.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well she\'92s not 
\f2\i now
\f1\i0 \'85 yet.\'94 Johnny recalled the obscene proposition these clowns had made Sarah: if it isn\'92t too much trouble, could we get you pregnant? But Johnny\'92s thought raced to an even more heinous offence. \'93You and your friends almost got me killed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That wasn\'92t us. That was PAPER. And it was one of my lot who got you out of there.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny didn\'92t recall it that way; he was clear he personally brokered a stand-off that night, which gave Sarah enough time to flee, but he admitted to himself things were hazy after an Elvi waitress had assaulted him. \'93So where\'92s the rest of your minstrel troupe then?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The others don\'92t know I\'92m here,\'94 Chilo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny sensed he was being sly. \'93What\'92s this about?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo pat the linen table and popped his lips a couple of times while he assembled his thoughts. \'93I understand you had a mishap recently.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny became steel, the way he knew he always could. \'93I\'92ve had no mishaps.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, I\'92d call blowing up a Shakti Research facility a mishap.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How do you know about that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo sat back, easing into his rehearsed agenda. \'93There were deaths.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No one important.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The coffee arrived. Johnny held his breath, as Chilo slurped it and earned disapproving murmurs from nearby tables.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Go on,\'94 Johnny said, defying Chilo to make matters worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You spun a Gyges disc.\'94 Chilo made it worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny felt his lungs turn to ice. His back prickled like metal fibres were growing out of it. Chilo let him sit in terror for as long as he dared. \'93Fear not, young Stoat. I\'92ve come to help you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny scoffed. \'93How can you help me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93When you spin a Gyges disc you create an astronomically complex environment. The laws of physics\'85\'94 Chilo studied Johnny and decided to dumb it down. \'93What you created in your lab was something like a very very\'85 very small black hole.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You mean it sucks like nothing else.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And there Chilo realised the error of crude analogies with this man, but was committed. \'93It would be more accurate to say it both sucks and blows.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But a black hole sucks everything in its wake.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A popular misconception, young Stoat. Black holes also emit radiation. You see, powerful quantum vacuum fluctuations convert into pairs of particles, one of which escapes the event horizon at the speed of infinity, while\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny regarded Chilo with impatient superiority. \'93You\'92re talking about Hawking radiation. Please don\'92t presume to talk down to me,\'94 he said, talking down to Chilo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Apologies.\'94 Chilo slurped at his coffee again while he reappraised his tactics. \'93Look, in the case of a Gyges disc, the accretion of energy is both more intense and more local to a four-dimensional physical space, and is, at least theoretically, limitless.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There is no such thing as limitless,\'94 Johnny said, feeling he was getting the better of this tussle of minds.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo shrugged. \'93Think of a Gyges disc as inevitably unstable. Spin it fast enough or for long enough, and the phenomena you experienced is guaranteed to occur.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny soured. \'93So the whole research project was doomed from the start.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo held his coffee cup in mid air and meditated upon it. He knew he could back out. Perhaps there\'92d be other opportunities, if only Kleoboulos wasn\'92t so sure the prophecy required certain events occur by Christmas. \'93Not necessarily.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hope cast its sunny aspect upon Johnny\'92s gloom. \'93What?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The reaction can be stabilised. You simply need something to suck everything the Gyges disc blows.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It sounded to Johnny like all the times he\'92d haggled with a sex worker. That never turned out well either. Incredulous, he blurted, \'93Where am I supposed to find a sucker like that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You need a binary system,\'94 Chilo said somewhat enigmatically, and waited to see if Johnny could follow the drift. He couldn\'92t, so Chilo pressed on. \'93If you precisely position another Gyges disc in relation to the first, and spin it at the same speed, it will suck what the first is blowing, while the first will suck what the second is blowing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So kinda like some cosmic sixty-nine event.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Quite,\'94 Chilo said. He blinked slowly and deliberately in the manner that was said to make friends with cats. He felt a wry smile on his face and wondered how it got there. \'93Think of it more like the shape of the symbol for infinity. Achieve that shape, energetically, between a binary relationship of discs, and you will achieve\'85\'94 Chilo reconsidered his words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A stable energetic state?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, suffice to say, we\'92ll all get what we richly deserve\'85 you included, of course.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny had always dreamed of getting what he deserved, especially richly. And he was only\'85 \'93Where am I going to find another Gyges disc?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo paused, then capitulated to his destiny. He reached into his pocket, withdrew something slim and dark. Johnny\'92s mouth fell agape when the leathery, scaly object flared metallic-shiny for a brief second. Chilo pushed it across the smooth linen to Johnny. \'93Here. Have mine.\'94
